module

public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Span.Defs

/-!
# A period lattice lies in the span of the translated periodic set

Let `X` be a subset of an additive group `E` that is invariant under translation by a subgroup
`Λ`, and let `x₀ ∈ X`.  Every period `l ∈ Λ` is the difference `(l + x₀) - x₀` of two points of
`X`, so `Λ` is contained in the subgroup generated by the translated set `X - x₀`.

In the uniqueness argument of Cohn and Elkies, `X` is the center set of a periodic packing with
period lattice `Λ` and `x₀` is a center.  The inclusion of `Λ` in the lattice generated by the
translated centers makes the quotient of that lattice by `Λ` finite
(`SphereCeti.ZLattice.finite_relativeQuotient`) and bounds the number of center orbits by the
index (`SphereCeti.ZLattice.card_le_relIndex`).

## Main statements

* `SphereCeti.ZLattice.le_span_image_sub`: a subgroup of periods of `X` is contained in the
  subgroup generated by `X - x₀` for any `x₀ ∈ X`.

## References

* H. Cohn and N. Elkies, *New upper bounds on sphere packings I*, Annals of Mathematics 157
  (2003), 689–714, Section 8.
-/

public section

namespace SphereCeti.ZLattice

/-- If `X` is invariant under translation by every element of `Λ` and `x₀ ∈ X`, then `Λ` is
contained in the subgroup generated by the translated set `X - x₀`. -/
theorem le_span_image_sub {E : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] {Λ : Submodule ℤ E} {X : Set E} {x₀ : E}
    (hX : ∀ ⦃l x⦄, l ∈ Λ → x ∈ X → l + x ∈ X) (hx₀ : x₀ ∈ X) :
    Λ ≤ Submodule.span ℤ ((fun x => x - x₀) '' X) := fun l hl =>
  -- The period `l` is the translate of the point `l + x₀ ∈ X`.
  Submodule.subset_span ⟨l + x₀, hX hl hx₀, add_sub_cancel_right l x₀⟩

end SphereCeti.ZLattice
